# Secrets and local state -- never commit.
.env.local
*.token
secrets.yaml

# The four reference repos are separate clones with their own history and their
# own licences. They are read as input and never committed here; the corpus is
# the derived artifact. Committing them would relicense nothing and would put
# four more .git trees inside this one.
/ressources/

# The unfiltered extraction. The committed `catalog/` holds facts for all four
# repos -- paths, ids, classes, vocabulary terms, entity identifiers -- and no
# field that can hold authored text. This directory holds the same records
# verbatim, including the aliases, display names, comments and YAML blocks of
# the two repos that grant nothing, and those are what a licence withholds
# rather than what it lets us keep. It exists so the local provenance gate can
# compare a shipped string against its source. It sits outside `catalog/`
# because every data file in `catalog/` must validate against a schema and this
# store is the one file the corpus validator is required to reject.
.local/

# Home Assistant runtime state for the dev container -- the database, logs,
# registries and the config onboarding writes. All of it is generated, none of
# it is authored, and it is ignored wholesale so a container run can never
# commit a token or a device registry. Our own configuration lives in this
# repository (`custom_components/`, `packs/`, `engine/`) and is never written
# here; the compose file mounts that directory as `/config` and mounts the
# repository's packages read-only beside it at `/openhouse-src`.
#
# `docker/ha-config/*` rather than `docker/ha-config/` so git still descends, and
# the one file below can be named back in. It is not runtime state: it is the
# third-party blueprint `tests/test_module_host.py` and `tests/test_pack_authoring.py`
# read as their fixture -- the real MarqBarq Dynamic Lighting document, branches,
# templates, per-branch variables and all, which a hand-made stand-in would pass
# while the real screen did not. The tests call it "the committed blueprint", so
# it has to be committed, and without it a fresh checkout fails every case built
# on it. The rest of `blueprints/`, and everything else in this directory, stays
# ignored.
docker/ha-config/*
!docker/ha-config/blueprints/
docker/ha-config/blueprints/*
!docker/ha-config/blueprints/automation/
docker/ha-config/blueprints/automation/*
!docker/ha-config/blueprints/automation/MarqBarq/
docker/ha-config/blueprints/automation/MarqBarq/*
!docker/ha-config/blueprints/automation/MarqBarq/dynamic-lighting.yaml

# Node-RED's own state: the flows this machine's editor holds, which include the
# ones Open House pushed for a module it no longer hosts. Same rule as
# `ha-config/` -- it is this machine's, not the repository's.
docker/node-red-data/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# Node / panel
node_modules/
panel/dist/

# The end-to-end run's screenshot: generated, written at the repository root by
# `panel/scripts/browser-e2e.mjs`, and read by nobody.
e2e-house.png

# The developers' own scratch: the one-off browser walks (`panel/scripts/_*.mjs`),
# the probe trees, and the working notes. Every one of them is scaffolding for a
# session rather than part of the product: the four scripts that *do* ship are
# `browser-e2e.mjs`, `browser-check.mjs`, `ws-probe.mjs` and `generate-types.mjs`,
# and the walks beside them are named with a leading underscore so this rule can
# single them out.
panel/scripts/_*.mjs
tmp-probe/
claudesession.txt
spec.txt
stuff to add.txt
pyright.probe.json

# This run's screenshots: written by the `panel/scripts/_*.mjs` walks into
# `shots/` while a session is looking at the house, and read by nobody after.
# The same rule `e2e-house.png` follows -- generated, not authored.
shots/

# The browser server's own scratch: the console logs, page snapshots and
# screenshots it drops beside the working directory on every walk. Generated,
# read by nobody, and committed once by accident -- hence the rule.
.playwright-mcp/

# The Store backend's own state -- the PocketBase database, uploads and logs of
# a Store somebody stood up from `store/`. Generated, and the thing that
# holds every publisher account, so it is never committed.
store/pb_data/

# The Store's admin address and password, which `docker compose` reads out of
# `store/.env` on every command. `store/.env.example` is the committed twin.
store/.env

# The bundled pack corpus: the room templates, the hand-written module packs and
# the generated `derived/` set. It is a working-tree artifact, not source -- this
# machine keeps it so the demo house and the packs suite have something to run
# against, and a fresh checkout deliberately ships none of it. Open House runs
# without it: a house is built from the blueprints a person imports, and the
# engine, the panel and the catalog all read the vocabulary from `catalog/`,
# which *is* committed.
#
# The three `example-*` documents stay, and so does the artefact `example-pack`
# pins. They are not shipped modules: they are the runtime schemas' own
# demonstrations, pinned by
# `registry/`, and used by the test suite as its fixture -- so they are source,
# and the one pack the project ships is deliberately the one that demonstrates
# the format rather than one that does anything.
/packs/derived/
/packs/official/*
!/packs/official/example-house.yaml
!/packs/official/example-export.yaml
!/packs/official/example-pack.yaml
!/packs/official/example_pack/
!/packs/official/HANDWRITTEN

# The bundled packs' own scenarios: one per shipped pack, each installing a pack
# this repository does not ship. Kept beside the corpus for the same reason, and
# ignored for the same reason -- a scenario whose `install_pack` step names a
# file no checkout has is a runner with nothing to run.
/scenarios/packs/
